{"id": "yph2_v1_u5_c2_pdf028_0116", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 5, "unit_name": "Further Mechanics", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "5B Circular Motion", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [28, 28], "char_count": 449, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nradianaunitof anglemeasurement,oneradianisequivalentto 57.3degrees\n\nangular displacement thevectormeasurement of theangle through whichsomethinghasmoved\n\nangularvelocity,wtherateatwhich the angulardisplacement changes;unit,radianspersecond\n\ncentripetal acceleration,a theaccelerationtowardsthecentre of a circle thatcorrespondsto thechangesindirectiontomaintain an object'smotionaroundthatcircle\n\n• centripetal acceleration,a=rw²"}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u5_c2_pdf029_0117", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 5, "unit_name": "Further Mechanics", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "5B Circular Motion", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [19, 19], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 281, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Explainthatacentripetal forceisrequiredtoproduceandmaintaincircularmotion.\n\n• Usetheequationsforcentripetalforce.\n\nInSection5B.1,wesawhowanobjectmovinginacirclemustbeconstantlyacceleratingtowards thecentreofthecircleinordertomaintainitsmotionaroundthecircle."}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u5_c2_pdf029_0118", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 5, "unit_name": "Further Mechanics", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "5B Circular Motion", "section": "WHYCIRCULARMOTION?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [19, 19], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 1181, "text": "WHYCIRCULARMOTION?\n\nWhenahammerthrowermovesanathleticshammeraroundinacircle,thehammerhasanangulan that it wasmovingat theinstant ofrelease.Thisdirectionis always alongthe edge ofthecircle(a tangent)atthepointwhenitwasreleased.\n\nfigATheinstantaneousvelocityofanobjectmovinginacircleistangentialtothecircle.Whenthereisnoresultant force,velocitywillbeconstant,soitmovesinastraightline,asthishammerwilldowhentheathleteletsgo.\n\nThehammer ismoved ina circle ata constantspeed andso themagnitudeof thevelocityis always thesame.However,the direction of thevelocity is constantly changing.Thismeans that thevector ofvelocityisconstantlychanging,andachangeinvelocityisan acceleration.Newton'sfirstlawtells usthataccelerationwill onlyhappenifthereisaresultantforce.Thehammerisconstantlybeing pulledtowardsthecentreofthecircle.Inthisexample,theforceprovidingthispullisthetensionin thestring（orchain).Foranyobjectmovinginacircle,theremustbearesultantforcetocausethis acceleration,and itiscalled thecentripetalforce.\n\nCENTRIPETALFORCE\n\nThemathematicalformulaforthecentripetalforceonanobjectmovinginacirclecanbefoundfrom Newton'ssecondlaw,andtheequationwealreadyhaveforthecentripetalacceleration:"}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u5_c2_pdf029_0120", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 5, "unit_name": "Further Mechanics", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "5B Circular Motion", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "learning_tip", "book_pages": [19, 19], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 346, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nThere isnospecialcentripetal force.Anyresultantforcethat makes an object move in a circleislabelledasthe centripetalforceforthatobject and circle.Forexample,a satellitewillmoveinacircular orbitaround theEarthbecause itsweightactstowardsthe centreof theEarth.Theweight istheonlyforceacting,socould bereferredtoasthecentripetal force."}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u5_c2_pdf029_0121", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 5, "unit_name": "Further Mechanics", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "5B Circular Motion", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [19, 19], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 128, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nMakesurethatyouknowthe differencesbetween angular velocityandspeedincircular motion,includingthecorrect unitsforeach."}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u5_c2_pdf030_0122", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 5, "unit_name": "Further Mechanics", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "5B Circular Motion", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [20, 20], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 765, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nAcentrifuge isamachine for spinning things.There is no 'centrifugal'force-donotuse thephrase'centrifugal force'\n\nWithouta centripetal force acting,Newton's1st lawwill makeanobjectcontinue ina straight line.This can confuse peopleinto thinkingthata force ispushingan objectoutwards. Infact,this showsalackof force;ifa forcewerepresent, thiscouldkeepit movingin a circle.\n\nfigBAstronauts aresubjecttoextremeaccelerationforces.Theseforcesaresimulated in trainingbythecentripetal force ina giant centrifuge.\n\nNoting thatv=rw,thereis an alternative equationforcentripetal force in terms of angularvelocity:\n\nTheresultantcentripetalforceneededwillbelargerif:\n\n• ·therotatingobjecthasmoremass\n\n• theobjectrotatesfaster\n\n• theobjectisclosertothecentreofthecircle"}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u5_c2_pdf030_0123", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 5, "unit_name": "Further Mechanics", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "5B Circular Motion", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [20, 20], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 659, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nEstimate the centripetal forceon anastronaut in the astronauttrainingcentrifuge(seefigB)if his capsulerotatedonce every2seconds.\n\nEstimate ofradiusof revolution:r=6m.\n\nEstimate of astronaut's mass:m=80kg\n\nThis is about6 times hisweight.\n\nIf theoperatorof thecentrifugeweretoincreaseitsrateofrotationtoonceeverysecond,whatwould the astronaut's angular velocity,centripetal force and acceleration nowbe?\n\nangularvelocity.w=2πf=6.28rads-1\n\ncentripetalforce,F=mrw²=（80)×（6)×（6.28）²=18900N\n\ncentripetalacceleration,a=rw²=（6)×（6.28)²=237ms-2\n\nThis is about 24 times the acceleration duetogravity andwould probably kilthe astronaut aftera fewseconds."}
