{"id": "yph2_v1_u6_c4_pdf056_0231", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 6, "unit_name": "Electric and Magnetic Fields", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "6B Capacitors", "section": "Investigatingcurrentflow through acapacitor", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [46, 47], "pdf_pages": [56, 57], "char_count": 1425, "text": "Investigatingcurrentflow through acapacitor\n\nfigAInvestigatinghowthecurrentthroughacapacitorchangesover time.\n\nYou caninvestigatehowthecurrentthroughacapacitorchangesovertimebyconnectingadatalogger, whichsensescurrentinserieswiththecapacitorandthenchargeanddischargeit.Asuitableset-upforthis isshowninfigA.Thisset-upcouldbealteredtologthepotentialdifferenceacrossthecapacitorovertime, usingavoltagesensorinparallelacrossthecapacitor.\n\nSafetyNote:Only use small capacitors as large charged capacitors cangivea severe electric shock.Do notoverchargethecapacitorsastheymayexplode.\n\nIfinfigA,thecapacitorisfullycharged,itwillbeat6V,andfromQ=Vwekowitwillbestoring 0.6mCofcharge.RecallthatinSection6B.1welookedathowtheelectronsinthecircuitare influencedbytheelectricfieldcausedbythesupplyvoltage,andtheirownmutualrepulsion.Ifthe two-wayswitchinfigAismovedtopositionB,theelectronsonthecapacitorwillbeabletomove tothepositivesideofitbydischargingvia thelamp.Asithas100Qresistance,theirprogresswillbe slowedbythelamp.buttheywill stilldischarge,and thelampwill lightforaslongassomecurrent flows through it.\n\nAtfirst,therushofelectronsasthecapacitordischargesisashighasitcanbe-thecurrentstartsat amaximum.WecancalculatethiscurrentusingOhm'slaw-itis0.06A.Aftersomeelectronshave discharged,thep.d.acrossthecapacitorisreduced andtheelectricfield,andthereforethepushon\n\ntheremainingelectrons,isweakerThecurrent islessandthelightwillbedimmer"}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u6_c4_pdf056_0232", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 6, "unit_name": "Electric and Magnetic Fields", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "6B Capacitors", "section": "Investigatingcurrentflow through acapacitor", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [46, 47], "pdf_pages": [56, 57], "char_count": 474, "text": "Sometimelater,theflowofelectronsissosmall thatthecurrentisdowntoatrickle,andthelamp willbesodimthatitmaylooklikeitisoff.Eventually,thecapacitorwillbefullydischargedandthere will benomoreelectronsmovingfromonesideofthecapacitortotheother-thecurrentwill be zero.Ifweputthisstorytogetherovertime,thedischargingcurrent,p.d.acrossthecapacitor,and chargeremainingonthecapacitorwill followthepatternsshown onthethreegraphsinfigB.\n\nfigBDischargecurvesfora capacitorthroughalightbulb"}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u6_c4_pdf057_0233", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 6, "unit_name": "Electric and Magnetic Fields", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "6B Capacitors", "section": "THETIMECONSTANT", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [47, 47], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 797, "text": "THETIMECONSTANT\n\nHowcouldwemake thelamplight upfor longer,given thesamepowersupply?There aretwo possibilities:\n\n• 1:storemorechargeonthecapacitor\n\n• 2decrease therate atwhich thecapacitor discharges.\n\nFor the samemaximump.d,increasing the capacitance,C,will increase the charge stored,as Q=V Alternatively,thechargewouldfowmoreslowlyifthebulb'sresistance,R,wasgreater\n\nAnoverallimpressionoftherateofdischargeofacapacitorcanbegainedbyworkingout the timeconstant,.Thisiscalculatedfrom=RC,andwithresistanceinohmsandcapacitancein farads,theanswerisinseconds.Infact,thetimeconstanttellsyouhowmanysecondsittakesforthe currenttofall to37%ofitsstartingvalue.Wewillseethemathematicsofhow37%comesabout inthenextsection,butfornowwejustneedtounderstandthatRCindicateshowquicklyacharged capacitorwilldischarge."}
