{"id": "yph2_v1_u8_c0_pdf139_0582", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 8, "unit_name": "Thermodynamics", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Chapter 8A Heat and Temperature thinking bigger", "section": "PHYSICS IN DETAIL", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [129, 129], "pdf_pages": [139, 139], "char_count": 1285, "text": "PHYSICS IN DETAIL\n\nNowwewilllookatthephysicsindetail.Someof thesequestionswill linktotopicselsewhere in thisbook,soyoumayneedtocombineconceptsfromdifferentareasofphysicstoworkout theanswers.\n\n• 3Explainwhyadetailedunderstandingof thegaslawsgoverningtheflightof theballoonis likelytobelessimportantto thesepilotsthan theirknowledgeofweathersystems.\n\n• 4(a)Draw a free-body forcediagram of theballoonat the momentof release at theground\n\n• (b)Explain how theforcesinyour diagram in(a)havemagnitudes that mean there isa resultant upwardforce.\n\n• 5Thistypeofballoonhasafixedvolume.\n\n• (a)Withreferencetoanyrelevantgas laws,explainwhythepressureof theairoutsidethe balloonfallsastheballoonrises.\n\n• (b)(i)Thisballoon,filledwithhelium,liftsoffwithavolumeof6200m²atground level (20°Cand apressureof101000Pa)andrisestoamaximumaltitudeof9100m (-44°℃and apressureof30100Pa).Ifnoheliumgasescapes,calculatethevolume of thegasintheballoonatthisnewaltitude.\n\n• (i)Infact,themaximumvolumeof theballoonatthe altitudeof9100m is9900m3 Explainwhyit doesnotexpand to thevolumeyoucalculated in(b)(i).\n\n• (c)Comparetheaveragekineticenergyofairmoleculesat9100maltitudewiththoseat groundlevel.\n\n• 6Asthe airinahotairballoon isheated,theballoonrises.Why did thesmoke inMontgolfier's fireplaceriseup thechimney?"}
