{"id": "yph2_v1_u10_c11_pdf160_0666", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 10, "unit_name": "Oscillations", "chapter_no": 11, "chapter_title": "10A Oscillations", "section": "CHAPTER 10A OSCILLATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [150, 150], "pdf_pages": [160, 160], "char_count": 1169, "text": "CHAPTER 10A OSCILLATIONS\n\nOscillations arecommon in everyday life,and studying them can be fun!The mathematicsgoverning these oscillationscouldbeconsidered lessfun,but actually ismucheasier thanitmay lookwhen all the equations are written outtogether.\n\nIn thischapterwewill concentrateon simpleharmonicoscillations,astheseare themostcommon and help to find useful solutions to engineering problems. We will look at how oscillations can be used for fun in everyday life,fromplayground toystonightclubmusic.\n\nOscillationscanalsogetoutofcontrol,whichcanbedamagingordangerous.nAngers,France,n1850,over 220 soldiersdied as thebridge theywere crossingcollapsed into the cold riverbelow.It isbelieved thatthe rhythmicmarchingofthesoldierscaused thebridge tooscillatetoomuch and thisbroke thecorrodedcables thatsupported it.Followingthisdisaster,andsomeothersimilarbridgecollapses,soldiers arenowtrainedto march outofstep acrossbridgesso asto avoidgenerating excessive oscillations.\n\nWe will see how such large oscillations develop and whatcanbe done to reduce theproblems they cause.These solutionsrange from carsuspension systemsto earthquakeprotectionsystems in skyscrapers."}
{"id": "yph2_v1_u10_c11_pdf160_0667", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 10, "unit_name": "Oscillations", "chapter_no": 11, "chapter_title": "10A Oscillations", "section": "MATHS SKILLS FOR THIS CHAPTER", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [150, 150], "pdf_pages": [160, 160], "char_count": 599, "text": "MATHS SKILLS FOR THIS CHAPTER\n\n• Draw and use the slope of a tangent to a curve as a measure of rate of change (e.g. drawing a velocity-time graph froma displacement-time graph)\n\n• Use of small angle approximations(e.g.in pendulum motion)\n\n• Changing the subject ofa non-linearequation(e.g.finding the gravitational field strength from apendulum)\n\n• Sketching and interpreting relationships shown graphically (e.g. sine and cosine functions for displacement, velocityandaccelerationofanoscillator)\n\n• Applyingtheprinciplesofcalculus(e.g.the connectionsbetween acceleration,velocity and displacement)"}
