{"id": "yph2_v1_u10_c11_pdf169_0699", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International A2 Level Physics Student Book 2", "author": "Miles Hudson", "unit_no": 10, "unit_name": "Oscillations", "chapter_no": 11, "chapter_title": "10A Oscillations", "section": "RESONANCE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [159, 159], "pdf_pages": [169, 169], "char_count": 797, "text": "RESONANCE\n\nIfasystemorobjectisforcedtovibrateatitsnaturalfrequency,itwill absorbmoreandmoreenergy noisyduringonepartofthewashcycle,themotorwill bespinningatacertainfrequency.Thisdrives vibrationsinthemachineatafrequencythatisthenaturalfrequencyofoneofthepanels,which thenvibrateswithverylargeamplitude,makingaloudnoise.Duringtherestofthewash,themotor's rotationgeneratesvibrationsatotherfrequenciesthatdonotmatchwiththenaturalfrequencyofany partof themachine,and it ismuchquieter.\n\nfigAGraphical illustrationofhowthedrivingfrequencyinawashingmachineaffectstheoscillationamplitudeof itssidepanel.Atallfrequencieshere,theamplitudeofforcedvibrationisthesame,andrelativelysmall.Whenthe motorsvibrationreachesabout11o0Hz,thegraphpeaks,showingthatthesidepanelvibratessignificantly.which willbeverynoisy."}
